{"result": {"response": {"id": "resp_054430a4e59b02ab0169bb19789ebc81919e53e8e15fcc993b", "created": 1773869432, "model": "gpt-5.2-2025-12-11", "object": "chat.completion", "system_fingerprint": null, "choices": [{"finish_reason": "stop", "index": 0, "message": {"content": "**CERVIX** is a structure of the female reproductive system located at the narrow, lower end of the uterus, where it connects to and is visible through the vagina. The cervix has a moist/inner lining surface that can be infected by HPV, and it is a principal site where HPV-related disease occurs. Most cervical dysplasia (precancer), as well as most cervical cancers, develop in the cervix—often in the transformation zone on the ectocervix—and cervical cancer types arising here include NECC.\n\nBecause **CERVIX** is central to cervical cancer prevention, diagnosis, staging, treatment, and surveillance, it is routinely examined and sampled. During a pelvic exam, the cervix can be visualized through the vagina. In screening (Pap testing/cervical cytology), cells are scraped from the cervix to detect cervical cancer or pre-cancer. When abnormalities are suspected, cervical tissue may be sampled for biopsy, including cervical biopsy and cone biopsy.\n\nIn cancer management, **CERVIX** is the key anatomic reference point for staging—determining whether disease is confined to the cervix (e.g., stage 1) or has extended beyond it—and for assessing involvement of tissues next to the cervix, including evaluation after surgery. Imaging such as MRI and ultrasound is used to evaluate the cervix and assess whether cancer has spread into adjacent tissues. The cervix is also a site where dye or a radioactive tracer may be injected to perform sentinel lymph node biopsy, reflecting how cancer can spread from the cervix to nearby lymph nodes.\n\nTreatment may involve partial removal of cervical tissue in very early disease, removal of the cervix via procedures such as cone biopsy or trachelectomy (fertility-sparing in selected cases), or removal of the cervix as part of a total/complete hysterectomy. In radiation therapy, **CERVIX** is a key target region and a reference for applicator positioning (for example, ensuring a ring applicator is pressed against the cervix). The cervix also plays a physiologic role in childbirth, as it opens and thins (dilates and effaces) during labor.", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, "provider_specific_fields": {"native_finish_reason": "stop"}}], "usage": {"completion_tokens": 442, "prompt_tokens": 618, "total_tokens": 1060, "completion_tokens_details": {"accepted_prediction_tokens": null, "audio_tokens": null, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "rejected_prediction_tokens": null, "text_tokens": null}, "prompt_tokens_details": {"audio_tokens": null, "cached_tokens": 0, "text_tokens": null, "image_tokens": null}}, "service_tier": null}}}